---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import QueueIsolationDiagram from '../components/QueueIsolationDiagram.astro'
import TokenRotationDiagram from '../components/TokenRotationDiagram.astro'

const meta = [
  { k: 'Role', v: 'Backend architecture & implementation' },
  { k: 'Stack', v: 'Node · TypeScript · PostgreSQL · Redis · BullMQ' },
  { k: 'Shape', v: 'AI journaling app · async agent pipelines' },
]
---

<Base title="Work · Nura" description="How I built Nura's backend: two isolated job queues with a recovery pass, and rotating auth tokens that catch a reused token and log the user out everywhere.">
  <article class="case">
    <header class="case__head shell">
      <p class="label">Case study / 01</p>
      <h1 class="display case__title">Nura</h1>
      <p class="case__standfirst measure">
        Nura is an AI journaling app, built on background agent jobs. The app
        itself is calm and almost wordless, but there's a lot going on
        underneath it. Two of those decisions matter most to me: how I keep the
        background work from taking itself down when something fails, and how I
        decide a session can still be trusted. Both are running below.
      </p>

      <dl class="case__meta panel">
        {meta.map((m) => (
          <div class="case__meta-item">
            <dt class="label">{m.k}</dt>
            <dd class="mono">{m.v}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </header>

    <!-- Diagram 1 -->
    <section class="case__section shell">
      <div class="case__lead measure">
        <p class="label case__index">Mechanism 01 / queue isolation</p>
        <h2 class="case__h2">One stuck job shouldn't freeze the whole app</h2>
        <p>
          When a session closes, two background jobs fire. One looks back over
          what happened in the session; the other generates an insight, but only
          when the user's patterns cross a threshold. I put these on
          <b>separate queues</b> on purpose. If the analysis job stalls, it stays
          on its own queue instead of starving insight generation, and a recovery
          pass comes back around for anything left stuck. For the user, one
          broken pipeline never freezes the rest of the app, and a late insight
          beats one that never comes. The diagram below runs the real
          thing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <QueueIsolationDiagram />
    </section>

    <!-- Diagram 2 -->
    <section class="case__section shell">
      <div class="case__lead measure">
        <p class="label case__index">Mechanism 02 / token rotation</p>
        <h2 class="case__h2">A stolen session shouldn't stay alive</h2>
        <p>
          Access uses a short-lived token. The long-lived one <b>rotates every
          time it's used</b>, and that rotation is what lets me catch theft. If a
          token that already rotated shows up again, two people are holding it,
          and one of them shouldn't be. So I revoke every session for that user
          at once, not just the suspicious request. Killing only the one request
          would leave the attacker a working token somewhere else. For the user,
          a leaked token can't hold a door open into their journal. The
          diagram catches a reuse as it happens.
        </p>
      </div>
      <TokenRotationDiagram />
    </section>

    <!-- system at a glance -->
    <section class="case__section shell">
      <div class="case__lead measure">
        <p class="label case__index">03 / the rest of the system</p>
        <h2 class="case__h2">The rest of the system, briefly</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="case__facts">
        <div class="case__fact panel">
          <p class="label">Provider abstraction</p>
          <p>Claude runs as the main model, with OpenRouter as a fallback and a mock version for tests. Switching models means changing one environment variable, and the code stays untouched. If the main provider has a bad day, the app keeps answering.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="case__fact panel">
          <p class="label">Context assembly</p>
          <p>Each user's context comes from their strongest patterns plus their most recent session summaries, all kept under a 4k-token budget. Every request logs what went into it. The model stays grounded in the user's real history without the cost creeping up.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="case__fact panel">
          <p class="label">Clean architecture</p>
          <p>The domain, application, infrastructure, and presentation layers stay separate, wired together with constructor injection. That keeps every use case testable without spinning up a database or an HTTP server.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="case__fact panel">
          <p class="label">Server-driven UI</p>
          <p>The app pulls its feature flags, config, and copy from a bootstrap endpoint when it starts. So rolling out a feature or fixing a bit of wording doesn't need an App Store update.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <aside class="case__note shell">
      <p class="label">Note</p>
      <p>The architecture here is real and running. Both diagrams model the actual mechanisms from the codebase, timings and all.</p>
    </aside>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case__head { padding-top: clamp(2rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 4rem); }
  .case__title {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
  }
  .case__standfirst {
    margin-top: clamp(1.2rem, 0.8rem + 2vw, 2rem);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--graphite);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .case__meta {
    margin-top: clamp(1.6rem, 1rem + 2vw, 2.6rem);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0;
  }
  .case__meta-item {
    flex: 1 1 14rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .case__meta-item:last-child { border-right: 0; }
  .case__meta-item dt { margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  .case__meta-item dd { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--ink); }

  .case__section { padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4.5rem); }
  .case__lead { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .case__index { display: block; margin-bottom: 0.8rem; color: var(--rust); }
  .case__h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .case__lead > p:not(.case__index):not(.case__h2) {
    color: var(--graphite); font-size: var(--step-0); line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .case__lead b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 500; }

  .case__facts {
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: clamp(1rem, 0.7rem + 1vw, 1.4rem);
  }
  .case__fact { padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem; }
  .case__fact p:first-child { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .case__fact p:last-child { color: var(--graphite); font-size: var(--step-0); line-height: 1.55; }

  .case__note {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 4rem);
    padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem;
    background: var(--invert-bg);
    color: var(--invert-fg);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }
  .case__note p:first-child { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; color: var(--rust); }
  .case__note p:last-child { color: var(--invert-mute); font-size: var(--step--1); line-height: 1.55; }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .case__facts { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .case__meta-item { flex-basis: 100%; border-right: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); }
    .case__meta-item:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  }
</style>
